@packages/core import { SigilCore }

component SigilProposalBaseSnapshot {
  goal {
    Construct one immutable, minimal proposal base from SigilCore-resolved
    workspace semantics plus repository evidence needed by agent evaluators.
  }

  interface {
    ProposalBaseSnapshot {
      construct(loadedWorkspace, destinationRoot) performs one attempt and returns a
      base fingerprint, selected-path manifest, and source-eligibility projection or
      rejects with COMPILER_UNSAFE_SNAPSHOT_PATH, COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED, or
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE
    }
  }
}

expand SigilProposalBaseSnapshot {
  logic {
    SnapshotSelection {
      Consume SigilCore discoverSigilWorkspace and loadSigilWorkspace results for
      ConfigFile identity, member boundaries, configured source membership, and
      optional GlossaryFile identity.

      Do not independently interpret ConfigFile discovery, include and exclude rules,
      nested workspaces, member roots, or glossary selection.

      In a Git workspace, add remaining repository evidence from tracked files and
      untracked files not excluded by effective Git ignore rules.

      When Git evidence is unavailable, apply proposal-fallback-exclusions-v1 to
      remaining evidence while retaining every SigilCore-resolved Sigil artifact.

      Revalidate the selected manifest and copied bytes after materialization. Reject
      this attempt with COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED when selected evidence changes.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    SnapshotSelection {
      SigilCore-resolved Sigil membership takes precedence over Git ignore rules.

      Repository metadata, ignored dependencies, generated output, caches, compiler
      runtime directories, and paths outside the workspace are not copied.

      A ConfigFile, GlossaryFile, or selected Sigil source whose path or traversed
      parent is a symbolic link rejects with COMPILER_UNSAFE_SNAPSHOT_PATH.

      Other symbolic links are excluded rather than materialized or followed.

      proposal-fallback-exclusions-v1 excludes non-Sigil evidence beneath any path
      segment named .git, .hg, .svn, node_modules, vendor, target, dist, build, out,
      coverage, .next, .nuxt, .turbo, .cache, .parcel-cache, .pytest_cache,
      __pycache__, .venv, venv, tmp, or temp.

      It also excludes .sigil/cache and compiler runtime or session artifacts. Retain
      every other regular file without traversing symbolic links.

      Selected-evidence mutation rejects this single attempt with
      COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED without exposing a partial base.

      Return a source-eligibility projection containing immutable-base source paths
      and the ConfigFile include, exclude, member-boundary, and workspace-containment
      rules needed to test hypothetical absent paths without rereading the workspace.
    }

    BaseSnapshotIdentity {
      The base fingerprint is SHA-256 over the canonical sigil-base-v1 binary
      encoding of the selected snapshot.

      Begin with the UTF-8 domain bytes sigil-base-v1. Encode every following field
      as a one-byte field tag, an unsigned 64-bit big-endian byte length, and the
      field bytes.

      Encode selected entries in normalized UTF-8 path order. Encode each path with
      slash separators and each content field with its exact copied bytes.

      Encode SigilCore source-selection data as UTF-8 canonical JSON with recursively
      sorted object keys, preserved array order, and no insignificant whitespace.

      Encode effective Git-ignore inputs in stable identity and normalized-path order
      using their exact bytes, including applicable repository exclude or global
      ignore inputs even when they are not copied into the snapshot.

      For a non-Git workspace, encode the exact UTF-8 identifier
      proposal-fallback-exclusions-v1 as the ignore-policy input.

      Timestamps, temporary paths, ownership metadata, and permissions do not
      participate.

      Equal base fingerprints mean every canonically encoded base input is
      byte-identical.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    MinimumProposalSnapshot {
      Decision: Combine SigilCore-resolved Sigil artifacts with tracked or
      non-ignored repository evidence rather than copying every filesystem entry.

      Scope: Governs base construction; excludes Sigil workspace membership.

      Trade-offs: Extra evidence selection depends on Git behavior and fixed fallback
      exclusions.

      Discarded alternatives: Applying Git ignore to configured Sigil was rejected
      because it would diverge from ordinary SigilCore resolution.

      Consequences: Proposal tools retain complete Sigil semantics with lower copy
      time and storage.
    }

    BaseSnapshotIdentity {
      Decision: Use a domain-separated, versioned, length-framed SHA-256 format for
      base identity.

      Scope: Governs base equality and staleness comparison, not confidentiality or
      authentication.

      Trade-offs: Exact-byte identity and encoding changes are
      compatibility-sensitive.

      Discarded alternatives: Unframed concatenation and normalized-content hashing
      were rejected as ambiguous or lossy.

      Consequences: Independent implementations reproduce the same base fingerprint
      from identical encoded inputs.

      Revisit when: SHA-256 support changes or a new encoding version is required.
    }
  }

  cases {
    SnapshotSelection {
      A configured Sigil source ignored by Git remains selected.

      A configured Sigil source reached through a symbolic-link parent rejects with
      COMPILER_UNSAFE_SNAPSHOT_PATH.

      An ignored dependency directory containing no configured Sigil is not copied.

      A tracked implementation or documentation file remains available as agent
      evidence.

      Source mutation during one copy attempt rejects that attempt with
      COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED.

      In a non-Git workspace, node_modules/pkg/index.js and .sigil/cache/report.json
      are excluded while src/index.ts and docs/design.md are retained.
    }
  }
}

component SigilProposalGeneration {
  goal {
    Validate, identify, and atomically activate each complete proposal override set
    against one immutable base snapshot.
  }

  interface {
    ProposalGenerationMaterialization {
      apply(baseFingerprint, sourceEligibility, proposal, activationCapability)
      allocates and atomically returns generation and proposal fingerprint or
      preserves the active generation and rejects with COMPILER_INVALID_PROPOSAL,
      COMPILER_GENERATION_EXHAUSTED, or COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE
    }

    ProposalGenerationActivationCapability {
      permits reading an immutable base source, resetting staging from the immutable
      base, writing or removing an eligible staged .sigil path, atomically activating
      staging, and discarding staging

      exposes neither a root path nor general filesystem authority
    }
  }
}

expand SigilProposalGeneration {
  logic {
    ProposalGenerationMaterialization {
      Allocate generation one first and exactly the preceding successful generation
      plus one thereafter.

      Use an unsigned 32-bit generation value with valid range 1 through 4294967295.

      Validate every proposal entry and construct the complete generation in staging
      before atomically replacing the active generation.

      Restore omitted replacements from the immutable base and remove omitted files
      that were additions in the preceding proposal.

      Preserve the active generation for every rejection and for later red, yellow,
      cancelled, or evaluator-failed compilation.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CompilationProposal {
      Proposal paths are normalized, workspace-relative, contained by the selected
      workspace, and end in .sigil.

      A replacement path is present in the immutable base source set. An addition
      path is absent from that set and is eligible only when the source-eligibility
      projection says the hypothetical path would be included by the base ConfigFile
      include, exclude, member-boundary, and workspace-containment rules.

      Proposals cannot change ConfigFile, GlossaryFile, implementation files,
      directories, symbolic links, or sources outside the configured workspace.

      Addition and replacement are supported. Deletion of a source present in the
      immutable base is not represented.

      Invalid path, content, or override shape rejects with
      COMPILER_INVALID_PROPOSAL.

      When the preceding successful generation is 4294967295, reject with
      COMPILER_GENERATION_EXHAUSTED before validation, staging, or any filesystem
      side effect.

      Staging or atomic-activation filesystem failure rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE.
    }

    ProposalSnapshotIdentity {
      The proposal fingerprint is SHA-256 over the canonical sigil-proposal-v1 binary
      encoding of the base fingerprint and complete override set.

      Begin with the UTF-8 domain bytes sigil-proposal-v1. Encode fields with the
      same one-byte tag, unsigned 64-bit big-endian length, and field-byte framing as
      the base format.

      Encode the base fingerprint as its exact 32 bytes. Encode proposal entries in
      normalized UTF-8 slash-separated path order with exact proposed UTF-8 bytes.

      Generation, baseEpoch, temporary paths, timestamps, ownership metadata, and
      permissions do not participate.

      Equal proposal fingerprints mean byte-identical complete overrides were applied
      to bases with equal base fingerprints.

      Fingerprints provide deterministic identity and integrity comparison, not
      confidentiality or authentication of an untrusted workspace.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    BaseSourceDeletion {
      Decision: Represent additions and replacements, but not deletion of a source
      present in the immutable base.

      Scope: Governs the source-text proposal map for the initial add-or-modify
      feature.

      Trade-offs: A design that removes a base source cannot yet be evaluated as one
      proposal.

      Design issues addressed: A map from path to complete source text has no
      unambiguous deletion value.

      Discarded alternatives: Empty text was rejected because it is valid source
      content; null and tombstone values were deferred because they expand the public
      proposal schema and fingerprint format beyond add-or-modify scope.

      Consequences: Omitting a base path restores its base content, while omitting a
      prior addition removes only that addition.

      Revisit when: Proposal compilation explicitly supports source deletion.
    }

    ProposalSnapshotIdentity {
      Decision: Use a domain-separated, versioned, length-framed SHA-256 format for
      complete proposal override identity.

      Scope: Governs proposal equality and report identity, not confidentiality or
      authentication.

      Trade-offs: Exact-byte identity and encoding changes are
      compatibility-sensitive.

      Discarded alternatives: Unframed concatenation, generation-only identity, and
      normalized-source hashing were rejected as ambiguous, session-local, or lossy.

      Consequences: Independent implementations reproduce the same proposal
      fingerprint from identical encoded inputs.

      Revisit when: SHA-256 support changes or a new encoding version is required.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationProposal {
      An empty proposal materializes the unchanged base as generation one with a
      deterministic proposal fingerprint.

      Replacing one source and adding another activates both atomically.

      Omitting a replaced base source restores it; omission never deletes that base
      source.

      An empty string is source content and does not represent deletion.

      Invalid proposal content rejects with COMPILER_INVALID_PROPOSAL.

      Exhausted generation rejects with COMPILER_GENERATION_EXHAUSTED.

      Filesystem activation failure rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE and
      preserves the preceding generation.
    }
  }
}

component SigilTemporaryWorkspace {
  goal {
    Own secure operating-system temporary storage, verified ownership markers, and
    recoverable cleanup for one proposal base epoch.
  }

  interface {
    TemporaryWorkspaceLifecycle {
      create(sessionIdentity) returns a private root or rejects with
      COMPILER_INVALID_SESSION_IDENTITY or COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE

      close removes the root, succeeds idempotently when already absent, and rejects
      with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED when ownership cannot be verified
      or COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE when cleanup otherwise fails

      verifyOwnership succeeds for the exact valid marker, rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED for missing or corrupt ownership
      evidence, or rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE when marker
      inspection encounters a filesystem or runtime failure
    }
  }
}

expand SigilTemporaryWorkspace {
  constraints {
    ProposalWorkspaceIsolation {
      Create an unpredictable directory with owner-only permissions.

      Atomically create an ownership marker containing the compiler format version
      and session identity before exposing the root.

      sessionIdentity is a canonical UUID version 4 string: exactly 36 lowercase
      ASCII characters in 8-4-4-4-12 form with valid version and RFC variant bits. Do
      not trim, case-fold, or otherwise normalize it.

      Invalid sessionIdentity rejects with COMPILER_INVALID_SESSION_IDENTITY before
      creating a directory.

      Encode the marker as UTF-8 canonical JSON with recursively sorted keys and no
      insignificant whitespace, containing numeric formatVersion and canonical
      sessionIdentity. Compare identity by exact UTF-8 bytes after validation.

      Preserve the marker after cleanup failure. Recovery removes only a directory
      whose marker is valid for that compiler format and session identity.

      A missing or corrupt marker is reported for manual inspection and never causes
      automatic directory removal.

      Expose the temporary root only to SigilProposalWorkspace for construction and
      evaluator-capability binding.
    }
  }

  cases {
    TemporaryWorkspaceLifecycle {
      Closing an already absent root succeeds.

      Cleanup failure preserves a valid marker for verified retry.

      Missing or corrupt marker prevents automatic recovery removal.
    }
  }
}

component SigilProposalWorkspace {
  goal {
    Coordinate cohesive base-snapshot, proposal-generation, and temporary-workspace
    owners behind one reusable lifecycle façade for SigilCompiler.

    Do not own compilation-session scheduling, evaluation, refresh, expiry, leases,
    or human approval.
  }

  interface {
    ProposalWorkspaceLifecycle {
      create(filesystem, workspaceOptions, sessionIdentity) returns
      ProposalWorkspaceAccess, base fingerprint, and selected-path manifest or
      rejects with ProposalWorkspaceFailure whose code is
      COMPILER_UNSAFE_SNAPSHOT_PATH, COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED,
      COMPILER_INVALID_SESSION_IDENTITY, COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE,
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED, or COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE

      apply(CompilationProposal proposal) returns ProposalWorkspaceAccess, allocated
      generation, and proposal fingerprint or preserves the preceding generation and
      rejects with ProposalWorkspaceFailure whose code is COMPILER_INVALID_PROPOSAL,
      COMPILER_GENERATION_EXHAUSTED, COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE,
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED, or COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE

      close removes the materialized workspace and succeeds idempotently when absent
      or rejects with ProposalWorkspaceFailure whose code is
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE, COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED, or
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE

      restore(filesystem, sessionIdentity, ProposalWorkspacePersistedState) verifies
      the existing ownership marker and materialized state and returns one restored
      SigilProposalWorkspace façade, one ProposalWorkspaceAccess, base fingerprint,
      selected-path manifest, and optional active generation and proposal fingerprint
      or rejects with COMPILER_INVALID_SESSION_IDENTITY, COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE,
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED, or COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE
    }

    ProposalWorkspaceFailure {
      category is proposal, caller-session, workspace-condition, or host

      code is the exact stable failure code emitted by the rejected operation
    }

    CompilationProposal {
      a proposal maps zero or more normalized workspace-relative .sigil paths to
      their complete resulting UTF-8 source text

      every proposal is a complete override set relative to one immutable base epoch
      rather than a delta from the preceding proposal
    }

    ProposalWorkspaceAccess {
      represents the immutable base view immediately after create or the allocated
      generation view immediately after apply

      coreFilesystem is a read-only filesystem capability for SigilCore

      agentRoot is an opaque evaluator binding that reveals no temporary path and
      requires the adapter to mount or sandbox the bound view read-only before access

      release idempotently revokes coreFilesystem and agentRoot

      after release, coreFilesystem and agentRoot reject with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE without reading the materialized workspace
    }

    ProposalWorkspacePersistedState {
      version 1 contains session identity, base fingerprint, selected-path manifest
      identity, source-eligibility identity, materialization identity, lifecycle
      state, optional active generation, and its optional proposal fingerprint

      contains no proposed source contents, credentials, temporary path, or writable
      filesystem capability
    }
  }
}

expand SigilProposalWorkspace {
  logic {
    ProposalWorkspaceLifecycle {
      For each create attempt, use the supplied filesystem and workspace options to
      rerun SigilCore discovery and loading, pass that exact loaded workspace result
      to one SigilProposalBaseSnapshot construction attempt, and do not require
      semantic resolution before copying the base.

      After a COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED attempt, first complete failed-create
      rollback. When rollback succeeds after attempt one or two, restart from
      discovery; after attempt three, return COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED.

      Begin in new and transition to active only after SigilTemporaryWorkspace and
      SigilProposalBaseSnapshot both succeed. Enter creation-cleanup-failed when
      failed-create rollback cannot remove a verified root, and enter
      active-cleanup-failed when cleanup after active use cannot remove one.

      Enter terminal manual-recovery-required when an existing root has a missing or
      corrupt ownership marker.

      On restore, validate ProposalWorkspacePersistedState version 1, persisted base
      identity, selected-path manifest, source eligibility, generation, proposal
      fingerprint, and active materialization before returning the restored façade
      and ProposalWorkspaceAccess. Never recopy the base, migrate an unsupported
      version, or infer missing persisted state.

      In active state, delegate proposal work to SigilProposalGeneration and return
      ProposalWorkspaceAccess for the resulting active generation.

      Retain the root writable filesystem authority inside SigilProposalWorkspace.
      During apply, give SigilProposalGeneration only a narrow internal staging and
      activation capability. Give SigilCore only coreFilesystem and give an evaluator
      only agentRoot through the adapter's enforced read-only sandbox.

      Treat the host process as trusted, but do not expose the writable capability or
      a temporary root path through ProposalWorkspaceAccess.

      Permit at most one live ProposalWorkspaceAccess handle. Reject a later apply or
      close with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE until the handle is released, without
      making SigilProposalWorkspace aware of session scheduling or leases.

      Before proposal validation, staging, or filesystem mutation, request
      SigilTemporaryWorkspace verifyOwnership.

      Ownership-unverified result enters manual-recovery-required and rejects apply
      without proposal work. Verification host failure preserves active and rejects
      apply so the caller may retry. Success permits proposal materialization.

      When failed-create rollback succeeds for a creation failure other than the
      first two COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED attempts, return the original creation
      failure, remain new, and permit a later caller retry.

      When failed-create rollback cannot verify ownership, return
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED with no related public context,
      preserve the root, and enter manual-recovery-required.

      When failed-create rollback otherwise fails, return
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE with no related public context, preserve the
      verified root, and enter creation-cleanup-failed.

      Retain only the original creation failure category and code in private memory
      while handling rollback. Do not return or serialize that context, and do not
      retain its message, proposed contents, or temporary paths.

      In creation-cleanup-failed, reject apply. Before create allocates another root,
      retry verified cleanup; successful cleanup returns to new and continues
      creation, while another cleanup failure preserves creation-cleanup-failed and
      rejects.

      Close in creation-cleanup-failed retries verified cleanup and reaches closed
      only after successful removal. Repeated create while active rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE.

      Every close-time COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE after active use enters
      active-cleanup-failed, including marker-read and pre-deletion failures. In that
      state, create and apply reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE and only close may
      retry.

      Close retry re-verifies ownership before deletion. Success reaches closed,
      another host failure preserves the current cleanup-failed state, and
      ownership-unverified enters manual-recovery-required.

      Apply in new or closed rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE. Close in new,
      active, or closed is idempotent and leaves the façade closed after successful
      cleanup.

      Create in closed rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE.

      Create, apply, and close in manual-recovery-required reject with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED. Never delete, replace, or expose
      another root from that façade.

      Automatic retry cannot leave manual-recovery-required. An operator must inspect
      and remove or quarantine the directory outside the session, abandon that
      session, and create a new session.

      In active-cleanup-failed, create and apply reject with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE. A later close may retry cleanup and reach closed but
      never restores active; the caller must create a new session.
    }

    FailureMapping {
      Return category proposal for COMPILER_INVALID_PROPOSAL and
      COMPILER_GENERATION_EXHAUSTED.

      Return category caller-session for COMPILER_INVALID_SESSION_IDENTITY and
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE.

      Return category workspace-condition for COMPILER_UNSAFE_SNAPSHOT_PATH,
      COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED, and COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED.

      Return category host only for COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ProposalWorkspaceDecomposition {
      Decision: Retain SigilProposalWorkspace as a façade over separate base,
      generation, and temporary-storage owners.

      Scope: Governs proposal materialization internals; excludes compiler session
      lifecycle and evaluator behavior.

      Trade-offs: Coordination uses explicit internal contracts and stable failure
      mapping.

      Design issues addressed: Workspace selection, fingerprint compatibility,
      generation activation, and secure cleanup change for different reasons.

      Discarded alternatives: One monolithic workspace owner was rejected because it
      couples repository policy, identity format, staging, and cleanup.

      Consequences: Each policy can be implemented and tested independently while
      SigilCompiler retains one lifecycle dependency.
    }

    ProposalWorkspaceCapabilityIsolation {
      Decision: Confine root writable proposal-workspace authority to the façade,
      delegate only a narrow internal staging and activation capability to
      SigilProposalGeneration during apply, and give SigilCore and evaluators
      separate read-only capabilities for the active view.

      Scope: Governs filesystem capability exposure after workspace creation and
      proposal activation; it does not claim isolation from the trusted host process.

      Assumptions: The host process and façade implementation are trusted, SigilCore
      honors its read-only filesystem capability, and the adapter enforces a
      read-only mount or sandbox before an evaluator can inspect agentRoot.

      Trade-offs: Capability adapters and sandbox setup add integration complexity,
      while keeping mutation authority out of compiler stages and agent tools.

      Design issues addressed: A compiler or evaluator that receives the writable
      root could mutate the proposal evidence during evaluation or bypass atomic
      generation activation.

      Discarded alternatives: Sharing one writable filesystem capability was rejected
      because convention alone cannot preserve the evaluation snapshot. Copying a
      second evaluator tree was rejected because it increases per-iteration I/O and
      lets deterministic and agentic stages observe different materializations.

      Consequences: SigilProposalGeneration remains the single staging and activation
      owner under the façade's lifecycle control. SigilCore and evaluators inspect
      the same active view without mutation authority, and adapters must provide the
      promised read-only enforcement.

      Revisit when: The host is no longer trusted, SigilCore requires controlled
      writes, or an adapter cannot enforce read-only evaluator access.
    }

    SingleLiveProposalWorkspaceAccess {
      Decision: Permit one live revocable ProposalWorkspaceAccess handle and reject
      apply or close until that handle is released.

      Scope: Governs read-view lifetime within one proposal-workspace façade; session
      operation scheduling remains owned by SigilCompilationSession.

      Assumptions: Deterministic and agentic stages for one evaluation must inspect
      one exact materialization, and the session releases access after those stages.

      Trade-offs: Callers must release access explicitly and can receive
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE for premature apply or close.

      Design issues addressed: Replacing or removing the active view while a reader
      remains live could mix generations or make reads depend on timing.

      Discarded alternatives: Multiple immutable generation handles were rejected
      because retaining complete trees defeats reuse and increases temporary storage.
      Automatic revocation was rejected because it can interrupt in-flight reads.
      Waiting inside the façade was rejected because scheduling belongs to the
      session and hidden blocking would make lifecycle timing nondeterministic.

      Consequences: One materialized tree serves one evaluation at a time; release is
      explicit, and later activation or cleanup begins only after readers finish.

      Revisit when: Versioned immutable views with bounded retention or reference
      counting can preserve exact-generation reads without repeated full copies.
    }

    ProposalFailureClassification {
      Decision: Preserve invalid proposal and generation exhaustion as proposal
      outcomes, lifecycle-state misuse as a caller/session outcome, unsafe, changing,
      or ownership conditions as workspace conditions, and only filesystem or runtime
      failures as host failures.

      Scope: Governs SigilProposalWorkspace rejection classification and stable-code
      compatibility.

      Trade-offs: Callers handle four failure categories but can choose retry and
      correction behavior from responsibility.

      Design issues addressed: Proposal correction, lifecycle misuse, workspace
      conditions, and environment failure require different attribution and recovery.

      Discarded alternatives: One generic failure category was rejected because it
      obscures responsibility and retry behavior.

      Consequences: Stable codes remain exhaustive while categories retain the
      glossary meaning of host failure.
    }

    BoundedSnapshotConstruction {
      Decision: Retry a concurrently changing base from fresh SigilCore discovery for
      three total attempts before returning COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED.

      Scope: Governs façade base creation; each SigilProposalBaseSnapshot
      construction remains one attempt.

      Assumptions: Most concurrent workspace edits settle within a small number of
      immediate reconstruction attempts.

      Trade-offs: Three attempts absorb short edits while bounding copy I/O and
      caller-visible latency.

      Discarded alternatives: Fail-fast was rejected as brittle during ordinary
      editing, and elapsed-time retry was rejected as nondeterministic.

      Consequences: Retry behavior and its terminal failure are reproducible.

      Revisit when: Measured workspace behavior supports another fixed bound.
    }

    RollbackFailurePrecedence {
      Decision: When create and rollback both fail, return the rollback failure and
      suppress the original creation failure from the public result. Ownership that
      cannot be verified returns COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED; every other
      cleanup failure returns COMPILER_WORKSPACE_HOST_FAILURE.

      Scope: Governs failure precedence and context retention during failed-create
      cleanup.

      Assumptions: Unremoved temporary storage is the immediate actionable recovery
      problem and may contain confidential proposal evidence.

      Trade-offs: Callers lose the original creation failure when cleanup also fails.

      Design issues addressed: Multi-cause errors can expose temporary paths, source
      contents, or inconsistent nested failure shapes.

      Discarded alternatives: Returning both full failures was rejected for
      confidentiality and protocol instability.

      Consequences: Successful rollback returns the creation failure except when a
      bounded changed-snapshot retry remains. Failed rollback returns its ownership
      or host cleanup failure while retaining only original category and code
      privately for correlation.

      Revisit when: A structured secure multi-cause error format is introduced.
    }

    OwnershipVerificationFailure {
      Decision: Treat missing or corrupt ownership evidence as terminal for the
      façade and require manual directory handling followed by session abandonment.

      Scope: Governs deletion authorization for proposal temporary roots whose marker
      cannot be verified.

      Assumptions: The ownership marker is the compiler's authorization boundary for
      deleting a directory outside the selected workspace.

      Trade-offs: A session cannot resume after marker loss even if an operator later
      removes or quarantines the directory.

      Design issues addressed: Reconstructing ownership evidence could authorize
      deletion of an unrelated or externally replaced directory.

      Discarded alternatives: Marker reconstruction and automatic deletion by path
      pattern were rejected because neither independently proves ownership.

      Consequences: The compiler fails closed, never removes unverifiable storage,
      and requires a new session after manual intervention.

      Revisit when: A stronger independently verifiable ownership mechanism is
      available.
    }

    TerminalProposalWorkspaceClose {
      Decision: Make successful close terminal for one SigilProposalWorkspace façade
      instance.

      Scope: Governs façade reuse after new, active, creation-cleanup-failed, or
      active-cleanup-failed lifecycle completion.

      Assumptions: One façade instance binds one ownership identity, base epoch, and
      internal eligibility and generation state.

      Trade-offs: Refresh constructs a replacement façade instead of resetting the
      existing instance.

      Design issues addressed: Reopening could mix lifecycle epochs, stale internal
      state, and a consumed temporary-root ownership identity.

      Discarded alternatives: Reset and reopen were rejected because they weaken the
      one-instance, one-epoch invariant.

      Consequences: Closed instances never allocate another root; refresh uses a new
      façade.
    }

    PersistentProposalWorkspaceRestoration {
      Decision: Reconstruct an active proposal-workspace façade from verified
      non-content session metadata and its existing private materialization.

      Scope: Governs cross-process session restoration; excludes creating a new base,
      repairing inconsistent state, or authorizing filesystem access.

      Trade-offs: Persisted lifecycle metadata becomes compatibility-sensitive.

      Design issues addressed: Cross-process callers require one exact restoration
      result and a deterministic compatibility boundary.

      Discarded alternatives: Recopying the selected workspace and storing proposed
      source contents in the session record were rejected because they change the
      frozen base or duplicate confidential material.

      Consequences: Independent CLI processes inspect the same base epoch and active
      generation while malformed, unsupported, or inconsistent version-1 state fails
      closed with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE and remains preserved for recovery.
    }

    EphemeralProposalWorkspace {
      Decision: Reuse one private operating-system temporary workspace across an
      explicit compilation session and update only affected proposal paths.

      Scope: Governs unwritten proposal evaluation; excludes ordinary compilation and
      approved repository mutation.

      Trade-offs: Local disk storage requires verified lifecycle and cleanup.

      Discarded alternatives: Prompt-only and compiler-memory overlays were rejected
      because ordinary agent and Sigil tools would inspect stale disk content.

      Consequences: Deterministic and agentic stages inspect the same snapshot while
      each base epoch is copied only once.
    }

  }

  cases {
    ProposalWorkspaceLifecycle {
      Successful create materializes one active base view and returns revocable
      read-only access, selected manifest, and base fingerprint. The first successful
      apply activates generation one in that workspace and returns its matching
      proposal fingerprint with new revocable read-only access.

      Released access rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE without reading the
      workspace. Apply or close while access remains live rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE; releasing that handle permits the operation.

      Apply before successful create rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE.

      Failed create remains new and a later create may succeed.

      Failed create whose rollback cleanup fails enters creation-cleanup-failed,
      blocks apply, and exposes no second root until verified cleanup succeeds.

      Create in creation-cleanup-failed retries cleanup before continuing; close in
      creation-cleanup-failed reaches closed only after successful cleanup.

      Cleanup failure after active use enters active-cleanup-failed, where create and
      apply reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE; successful close retry reaches
      closed and requires a new session rather than resuming evaluation.

      A close-time marker-read or other host failure after active use enters
      active-cleanup-failed. Create and apply reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE;
      close retry either reaches closed, remains active-cleanup-failed on host
      failure, or enters manual-recovery-required when ownership cannot be verified.

      Repeated create while active rejects without leaking or replacing the root.

      Apply after close rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE.

      Create after close rejects with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE and does not allocate
      another root.

      A missing or corrupt marker enters manual-recovery-required; create, apply, and
      close reject with COMPILER_WORKSPACE_OWNERSHIP_UNVERIFIED until an operator
      handles the directory and abandons the session.

      Apply with a valid ownership marker continues to proposal validation. Missing
      or corrupt marker performs no proposal work and enters
      manual-recovery-required. Marker-read host failure preserves active and permits
      a later apply retry.

      Three consecutive changed-snapshot attempts return COMPILER_SNAPSHOT_CHANGED;
      each internal base construction performs one attempt, and attempts two and
      three begin only after successful rollback of the preceding attempt.

      Close before create and repeated close both succeed and leave the façade
      closed.

      Restoring verified version-1 active state returns one façade, one revocable
      read-only access, base fingerprint, selected manifest, and matching optional
      generation identity without copying source content.

      Restoring mismatched persisted identity or generation metadata rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE and exposes no access.

      Restoring a malformed or unsupported persisted-state version rejects with
      COMPILER_WORKSPACE_STATE, performs no migration, and preserves the
      materialization for verified cleanup.
    }
  }
}
